# Runtime install of this project's own skill, plus canary agents and host
# settings. `workforce/` is the source distribution; `.claude/` is a working copy.
/.claude/

# ANCHORED above, and this is load-bearing. As a bare `.claude/` the pattern
# matched at EVERY depth, so it swallowed `fixtures/*/.claude/` — the trees the
# fixtures ARE. Twelve fixtures were committed as a CLAUDE.md and a TRUTH.md
# describing content that was never in the repo, and `bin/conformance` — a gate
# that exits 1 — failed 41 of 60 assertions on a fresh clone while passing here.
# Measured 2026-08-01 by cloning. The corpus is the only instrument that shows
# this system generalizes; ignoring it made a collaborator's checkout unable to
# run the one gate that says so.
!fixtures/*/.claude/

# AND the settings files inside a script fixture's tree, which sit one level deeper
# at `fixtures/scripts/<name>/.claude/`. A settings file is exactly what several of
# these fixtures ARE — `saunwire-*` seeds one with the user's own hooks and style so
# the reversal can be proven conservative — and a very common GLOBAL ignore
# (`~/.config/git/ignore`) excludes `**/.claude/settings.local.json` on developer
# machines. A repo `.gitignore` is read after the global one and wins, so the
# negation belongs here. Without it the trees are untracked, absent from every
# clone, and `bin/script-conformance` reports "fixture tree missing" there while
# passing on the machine that wrote them — the same failure the anchor above
# records, one directory deeper. Measured 2026-09-07.
!fixtures/*/*/.claude/
!fixtures/*/*/.claude/**

# NOTE: CLAUDE.md is deliberately NOT ignored here, diverging from claude-enforcer.
# Anyone working on this repo needs the orientation, and for a repo-only session —
# remote execution, a collaborator's checkout — the clone is the only copy there is.

# Host-generated snapshots and restore kits. Never committed.
.claude-backups/

# OS and editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~

# Python bytecode. `workforce/bin/` ships ~1,000-line stdlib scripts; a `py_compile`
# or import drops a `__pycache__/*.pyc` there, and `bin/check`'s "no shipped file is
# undeclared" gate goes red on it — worse, an uncaught .pyc could be committed and
# then shipped-but-undeclared. Ignored everywhere; a .pyc is never source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# fixture git histories are recreated by fixtures/init-git.sh, never committed
fixtures/*/.git/
